<%# THE CELEBRATION RELAY — one thing a redirect cannot destroy.

    WHAT IT IS FOR. On the inline transport the document that starts an on-chain
    flow is still alive when the server answers, so it paints the outcome itself.
    On the redirect transport that document is DEAD: the wallet app took over,
    the answer lands on studio-engine's callback page, and the callback ends with
    a hard navigation to wherever the flow said to land. Everything the original
    document would have painted is skipped, because there is no longer a
    component to paint it.

    This is the piece of work shared/_username_rename_intent named and deferred:
    "Reproducing the celebration would mean carrying its payload across the
    journal and re-staging it on arrival, which is a separate piece of work."
    So the relay is built as a PRIMITIVE with a kind, not as a contest-entry
    one-off. A flow adopts it in two moves: stash its payload under its own kind
    on the leg that cannot paint, and register a painter for that kind.

    WHAT IS LOST WITHOUT IT, and it is not decoration. The contest entry that
    prompted this carried three acts on its inline return leg, and two of them
    leave the UI asserting something FALSE when they are skipped: the free-entry
    token count never comes down (nothing else lowers it), and the navbar seeds
    and level stay at their pre-entry figures. The third is the celebration card
    itself. Measured on QA iPhone Safari 2026-09-09: entry recorded on chain,
    screen showed nothing.

    localStorage, NOT sessionStorage, for the reason walletJournal already
    settled: iOS does not guarantee a universal link returns to the originating
    tab, and sessionStorage is per-tab. It would be empty exactly when it
    mattered.

    SINGLE-USE AND EXPIRING, both deliberate and both copied from walletJournal.
    take() reads and clears in one motion, so a reload or a double-fired listener
    cannot throw the same party twice; the expiry means a slot abandoned midway
    cannot congratulate someone tomorrow for an entry they made today. %>
<script>
(function (W) {
  'use strict';

  // ONE SLOT. A browser runs one wallet round trip at a time — the journal this
  // rides beside is itself a single slot — so a queue would be structure with no
  // case behind it. The kind on the record is what keeps two flows from reading
  // each other's payload, and clear() is therefore also the purge: there is no
  // second key to leave behind.
  var KEY = 'tm_celebration_relay';

  // Ten minutes, the same bound walletJournal chose and for the same reason: long
  // enough to outlast a human reading a wallet approval screen, short enough that
  // an abandoned trip is gone before it can be replayed by accident. The relay is
  // written AFTER the wallet has answered, so in practice it lives for one
  // navigation; the bound is for the trip that never lands.
  var MAX_AGE_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000;

  // EVERY access guarded. localStorage throws outright in a Safari private window
  // and in some embedded webviews, which are precisely the browsers a mobile
  // wallet flow runs in. A storage failure must degrade to "no celebration
  // pending" and never to an exception thrown out of a page load.
  function store() {
    try {
      return W.localStorage || null;
    } catch (e) {
      return null;
    }
  }

  function clear() {
    var s = store();
    if (!s) return;
    try { s.removeItem(KEY); } catch (e) { /* nothing to do and nothing to say */ }
  }

  // The raw record, or null. Expiry and corruption are ANSWERS here, not errors:
  // both clear the slot so the next read reports "nothing pending" rather than
  // re-deciding the same thing forever.
  function record() {
    var s = store();
    if (!s) return null;

    var raw;
    try {
      raw = s.getItem(KEY);
    } catch (e) {
      return null;
    }
    if (!raw) return null;

    var rec;
    try {
      rec = JSON.parse(raw);
    } catch (e) {
      clear();
      return null;
    }

    if (!rec || typeof rec !== 'object' || !rec.kind) { clear(); return null; }

    if (typeof rec.stashedAt === 'number' && (Date.now() - rec.stashedAt) > MAX_AGE_MS) {
      clear();
      return null;
    }

    return rec;
  }

  // Write the payload the landing page will need. Answers whether it was
  // actually persisted, so a caller that cares can say so rather than promise a
  // celebration the device cannot deliver.
  function stash(kind, payload) {
    var s = store();
    if (!s || !kind || !payload) return false;
    try {
      s.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify({ kind: String(kind), payload: payload, stashedAt: Date.now() }));
      return true;
    } catch (e) {
      // Quota, private mode, or a disabled store. The flow itself has already
      // succeeded on the server by the time anything is stashed, so a failure
      // here costs the party and nothing else.
      return false;
    }
  }

  // Read WITHOUT clearing, and only for the kind asked for. A slot holding
  // ANOTHER flow's payload answers null and is LEFT ALONE — the flow that owns it
  // is the one entitled to consume it.
  function peek(kind) {
    var rec = record();
    return (rec && rec.kind === String(kind)) ? rec.payload : null;
  }

  // Read and clear in one motion. This is the single-use property: a reload, a
  // back button, or a listener that fires twice finds an empty slot.
  function take(kind) {
    var payload = peek(kind);
    if (payload) clear();
    return payload;
  }

  var painters = {};

  // Register the painter for a kind. Idempotent by assignment, because the
  // partials that call this are rendered on every document and a second
  // registration of the same function is not an error.
  function define(kind, painter) {
    if (!kind || typeof painter !== 'function') return;
    painters[String(kind)] = painter;
  }

  // Paint whatever is waiting, at most once. Returns the kind painted, or null.
  //
  // TAKE BEFORE PAINT, and the order is the guarantee: a painter that throws has
  // already consumed the slot, so a broken painter costs one celebration rather
  // than repeating on every page for ten minutes.
  //
  // AN UNREGISTERED KIND IS LEFT WHERE IT IS. The painter for a flow ships in a
  // partial the layout renders, so its absence means this document genuinely
  // cannot paint that kind — not that the payload is spent. The expiry bounds
  // how long it waits for a document that can.
  function drain() {
    var rec = record();
    if (!rec) return null;

    var painter = painters[rec.kind];
    if (!painter) return null;

    var payload = take(rec.kind);
    if (!payload) return null;

    try {
      painter(payload);
    } catch (e) {
      try { console.warn('[celebration-relay] painter for "' + rec.kind + '" failed:', e); } catch (_) {}
    }
    return rec.kind;
  }

  W.tmCelebrationRelay = {
    KEY: KEY,
    MAX_AGE_MS: MAX_AGE_MS,
    stash: stash,
    peek: peek,
    take: take,
    clear: clear,
    define: define,
    drain: drain
  };

  // === WHEN THE DRAIN RUNS ==================================================
  //
  // Every painter this relay serves ends up writing to an Alpine store, and the
  // stores register during Alpine's own boot. So the drain waits for Alpine to
  // have STARTED (alpine:initialized fires after the DOM walk, which is when the
  // modal host's own listeners are wired) rather than for DOMContentLoaded,
  // which is earlier than either.
  //
  // AND FOR StateFanout, which is a DEFERRED importmap module: the layout's own
  // note records that Alpine's defer script fires alpine:init BEFORE importmap
  // modules execute, so a drain that fired at the first opportunity could reach
  // the seeds fanout before it exists. The wait is BOUNDED and then paints
  // anyway — a missing fanout must cost the seeds animation, never the card.
  //
  // The document this runs on is not necessarily a wallet callback or a contest
  // page. On a document with an empty slot every line below is a no-op, which is
  // why rendering this everywhere is safe.
  function alpineStarted() {
    return !!(W.Alpine && W.Alpine.version);
  }

  function fanoutReady() {
    return !!(W.StateFanout && typeof W.StateFanout.apply === 'function');
  }

  function drainWhenReady() {
    // Nothing waiting: answer immediately and schedule nothing. This is the
    // common case on every page in the app.
    if (!record()) return;

    var attempts = 0;
    (function attempt() {
      // ~3s at 50ms. Long enough for a module graph on a cold phone connection,
      // short enough that the card is not visibly late when one never arrives.
      if ((alpineStarted() && fanoutReady()) || attempts++ >= 60) {
        if (alpineStarted()) drain();
        return;
      }
      W.setTimeout(attempt, 50);
    })();
  }

  function schedule() {
    if (alpineStarted()) drainWhenReady();
    else W.document.addEventListener('alpine:initialized', drainWhenReady, { once: true });
  }

  if (W.document.readyState === 'loading') {
    W.document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', schedule, { once: true });
  } else {
    schedule();
  }
  // A Turbo visit does not re-run this script, and the redirect leg lands with a
  // full document load — but a flow whose landing page is reached through Turbo
  // would otherwise never drain. take() keeps it to once either way.
  W.document.addEventListener('turbo:load', drainWhenReady);
})(window);
</script>
